--TEST--
A `Core\Compress\Decompressor`'s bound is the whole stream's — the same frame is refused at the same ceiling however many chunks it arrived in, and both halves are asserted either side of their boundary — `rule:core-classes/decompression-bound`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// 16 KiB of one octet: the shape a decompression bomb has, small on the wire
// and large in the heap. A bound applied per `add` rather than per stream would
// not be a bound at all — ten chunks would each be given the whole ceiling — so
// what is asserted here is that the chunking buys nothing.
bytes $payload = Core\Str::repeat("A", 16384) as bytes;
bytes $frame = Core\Compress::compress($payload, Core\Codec::Gzip);
int $size = Core\Bytes::length($frame) as int;
array<int> $splits = [1, 2, 4, 8];

// The absolute half, on both sides of its boundary and under every chunking.
// The output's own size is accepted whole; one octet short of it refuses. A
// stream that charged each chunk its own ceiling would accept the eight-chunk
// row at 16383, and a stream that stopped one octet early would refuse the
// 16384 row — neither shows up in a single-chunk test.
int $atTheBound = 0;
int $underIt = 0;
foreach ($splits as int $pieces) {
    int $each = Core\Math::intDiv($size, $pieces);
    var $exact = Core\Compress::decompressor(Core\Codec::Gzip, 16384);
    var $short = Core\Compress::decompressor(Core\Codec::Gzip, 16383);
    int $at = 0;
    for ($at = 0; $at < $pieces; $at = $at + 1) {
        int $length = $at == $pieces - 1 ? $size - $at * $each : $each;
        $exact->add(Core\Bytes::slice($frame, $at * $each, $length));
        $short->add(Core\Bytes::slice($frame, $at * $each, $length));
    }
    if (Core\Bytes::length($exact->finish()) == 16384) {
        $atTheBound = $atTheBound + 1;
    }
    try {
        $short->finish();
    } catch (ParseError $over) {
        $underIt = $underIt + 1;
    }
}
echo "chunkings accepted at 16384: ", $atTheBound, "\n";
echo "chunkings refused at 16383: ", $underIt, "\n";

// The ratio half, with the absolute half wide open, measured against everything
// the stream was fed rather than against the last chunk: a frame of this size
// at 1000:1 has room and at 100:1 has none, in one chunk or in four.
int $roomy = 0;
int $tight = 0;
foreach ($splits as int $pieces) {
    var $wide = Core\Compress::decompressor(Core\Codec::Gzip, 1048576, 1000);
    var $narrow = Core\Compress::decompressor(Core\Codec::Gzip, 1048576, 100);
    $wide->add($frame);
    $narrow->add($frame);
    if (Core\Bytes::length($wide->finish()) == 16384) {
        $roomy = $roomy + 1;
    }
    try {
        $narrow->finish();
    } catch (ParseError $thin) {
        $tight = $tight + 1;
    }
}
echo "accepted at 1000:1: ", $roomy, "\n";
echo "refused at 100:1: ", $tight, "\n";

// A frame a compressing stream wrote is bounded exactly as one the whole-buffer
// member wrote. The bound is a property of the octets coming out, so which
// producer wrote the input is not a question it asks — a decompressor that read
// the producer would be two policies where the rule states one.
var $written = Core\Compress::compressor(Core\Codec::Gzip);
$written->add(Core\Bytes::slice($payload, 0, 8192));
$written->add(Core\Bytes::slice($payload, 8192, 8192));
var $bounded = Core\Compress::decompressor(Core\Codec::Gzip, 16383);
$bounded->add($written->finish());
try {
    $bounded->finish();
    echo "a streamed frame was not bounded\n";
} catch (ParseError $same) {
    echo "a streamed frame is bounded the same\n";
}

// And the refusal is the codec's own, naming the stream's member rather than
// the whole-buffer one — a program told to lower `$maxBytes` on a member it
// never called cannot act on the message.
var $named = Core\Compress::decompressor(Core\Codec::Gzip, 1024);
$named->add($frame);
try {
    $named->finish();
    echo "the bound did not hold\n";
} catch (ParseError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
chunkings accepted at 16384: 4
chunkings refused at 16383: 4
accepted at 1000:1: 4
refused at 100:1: 4
a streamed frame is bounded the same
Core\Compress\Decompressor::finish(): a `Core\Codec::Gzip` frame decompressing past the 1024 octet ceiling is refused rather than truncated (rule:core-classes/decompression-bound). `[limits] max_decompressed` and `[limits] max_decompression_ratio` are the operator's ceiling, which a call naming `$maxBytes` or `$maxRatio` lowers and nothing raises.
